{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011Cf1qowALYhpia586noFBk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```json\n{\n  \"final_answer\": \"mechanism_vs_evidence\",\n  \"evidence_tier\": \"animal\",\n  \"conclusion_tier\": \"human_therapeutic\",\n  \"bridge_supported\": false,\n  \"assumptions\": [\n    \"Mouse tumor models (xenografts and syngeneic) accurately predict human patient responses\",\n    \"In vitro macrophage phagocytosis translates to therapeutic benefit in humans\",\n    \"Tumor growth inhibition and mouse survival in preclinical models indicate human survival improvement\"\n  ],\n  \"steps\": [\n    \"Mechanism established: CD47-SIRP-alpha interaction blocks phagocytosis\",\n    \"In vitro proof-of-concept: anti-CD47 enables macrophage engulfment of human tumor cells\",\n    \"Preclinical efficacy: antibody treatment improves survival in mouse xenograft and syngeneic models\",\n    \"Claim extrapolates to human patient survival without clinical trial data\"\n  ],\n  \"alternative_hypotheses\": [\n    \"Mouse models may not recapitulate human immune system complexity and tumor microenvironment\",\n    \"Xenograft-derived results may not translate to spontaneous human tumors\",\n    \"Immunodeficient mice lack key immune components relevant to human anti-tumor immunity\"\n  ],\n  \"uncertainties\": [\n    \"No human clinical trial data presented; only preclinical mouse studies\",\n    \"Human patient outcomes not measured; only tissue expression and xenograft establishment described\",\n    \"Generalization across 'solid tumors' not supported by evidence limited to breast cancer models\"\n  ],\n  \"key_claims\": [\n    \"Anti-CD47 treatment improves survival in human patients with solid tumors\",\n    \"Evidence is restricted to mouse models and in vitro systems\",\n    \"Human clinical efficacy is not demonstrated\"\n  ],\n  \"self_check\": [\n    \"The claim makes a human therapeutic outcome assertion (improved patient survival)\",\n    \"The evidence tier is animal (mouse xenografts and syngeneic models)\",\n    \"A mechanistic pathway (CD47 blockade → macrophage phagocytosis) is used to bridge from preclinical to clinical claims\",\n    \"This is a classic mechanism_vs_evidence error: animal model efficacy is used to imply human clinical benefit without clinical trial data\",\n    \"The gap between mouse survival and human patient survival is substantial and not bridged by the supplied evidence\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": 0.92\n}\n```\n\n**Rationale:** The claim asserts that anti-CD47 treatment \"improves survival in human patients,\" but the evidence packet contains only preclinical data: in vitro macrophage assays, mouse xenograft studies, and mouse syngeneic tumor models. No human clinical trial results, patient survival data, or prospective human outcomes are presented. The described biological mechanism (CD47-SIRP-alpha blockade enabling phagocytosis) is used to justify the leap from mouse efficacy to human therapeutic benefit—a hallmark of mechanism_vs_evidence error. While the preclinical work is solid, the conclusion substantially exceeds what the evidence supports."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":856,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":727,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}}